{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghJDJpNDw7MkRoXLwqMx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QCgFLoHGBtqbrnLdfDQQ6e","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-215911__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","limit":250},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5041,"cache_read_input_tokens":19349,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5041},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"d6550729-6ee7-418b-8220-7caed6efe69b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:59:24.952Z","request_id":"req_011CghJDJZFLyrKUM6DiUGrp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QCgFLoHGBtqbrnLdfDQQ6e":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-215911__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","limit":250}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QCgFLoHGBtqbrnLdfDQQ6e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Dragon Quilt Builders\n2\t\n3\t*A build plan for an original block-building action RPG inspired by Dragon Quest Builders.*\n4\tWritten for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from `TASK.md` and this file alone.\n5\tPlan date: 2026-11-03. Engine: my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit `4390a98`). AI Hub client: session.js 1.4.0 (ai-hub commit `f8835e48`).\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## 0. At a glance\n10\t\n11\t| | |\n12\t|---|---|\n13\t| **Title** | **Dragon Quilt Builders** (picked by RNG; see §1) |\n14\t| **Pitch** | The patchwork realm came apart at the seams when the dragon **Frayvern** pulled out its Golden Thread and swallowed everyone's *Knack*, the gift of making things. You are the last **Builder**. Break blocks, gather materials, get *Notions* (ideas for recipes), craft, and build rooms block by block. Win the hearts of quirky residents, raise the town of **Thimbleton**, defend it from a monster siege, and unpick the knotted boss **Gnarlgrim**. |\n15\t| **Genre** | Sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting and town building. Same family as the source: real-time combat and a story told in chapters. |\n16\t| **Perspective** | Third-person **isometric 2.5D** over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90° steps (Q/E or the right stick) and zooms. **Three-quarter** is an optional view in Settings. Characters are the engine's 3D puppet rigs drawn as pixel art. Why iso: it shows two side faces and the top of every block, so 3D building reads clearly with a fixed camera, and it is the engine's native strength. |\n17\t| **Core loop** | Explore → break blocks and gather → get a Notion → craft at a station → build rooms and Patterns → residents move in and give Cheer → town levels up and unlocks more → the story pushes you into harder regions and finally a siege and a boss. |\n18\t| **Demo scope** | \"Chapter One: A Stitch in Time\", about 60–75 minutes for a new player. It has 5 residents, 6 regions, 8 room types, 30+ block and object types, 17 quests, a mini-boss, a siege with 3 waves and a final boss. After the credits, free play continues. A co-op **Quilting Bee** round (AI Hub shared world, 1–4 players) is also playable solo from the title. |\n19\t| **Deliverable** | One self-contained HTML file in this folder's root: `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html`. Dev files live in the subfolder of the same name. `exit-interview.json` sits beside the HTML. |\n20\t\n21\tThe source's distinctive elements, and what each becomes here (all names, characters, art, music and places are original):\n22\t\n23\t| Dragon Quest Builders | Dragon Quilt Builders |\n24\t|---|---|\n25\t| The dark lord took the power to build; you are the legendary Builder | Frayvern swallowed the *Knack*; only you can still make things. NPCs marvel at beds and doors (\"You *made* that? On purpose?!\") |\n26\t| A goddess guides you as a voice from the sky (Rubiss) | **Granny Gossamer**, the Great Quilter who stitched the world: a warm, scatterbrained voice in golden dialog boxes |\n27\t| Banner of Hope founds a base; base level rises | **Banner of Mending**: planting it stitches a glowing border on the ground. **Cheer** points raise the **Town Level** (1–5), which widens the border and unlocks recipes |\n28\t| Inspiration: touching new materials gives recipe ideas | **Notions** (\"You've got a notion!\"; *notions* is the sewing-shop word for haberdashery) |\n29\t| Rooms recognised from walls ≥2 high, a door and a light, then typed by their furniture | Same rule. 8 room recipes (Bedroom, Workshop, Kitchen, Mess Hall, Smithy, Guard Post, Snug, Quilters' Hall) |\n30\t| Blueprints that residents help build | **Patterns**: ghost-block layouts. Residents take materials from the Town Chest and place blocks too |\n31\t| Resident requests (the \"!\" over their heads), gratitude hearts | Requests with \"!\" balloons. **Cheer bubbles** (hearts) float over happy residents; walk through them to collect |\n32\t| Hunger, day/night, sleeping in beds | **Belly** meter, a 12-minute day, sleep through the night in a bed |\n33\t| Weapons double as block-breaking tools with tiers | Darning Stick → Cobble Club → Copper Bodkin → **Seam Ripper**, the only tool that cuts Black Knots |\n34\t| Base-defence sieges; bosses beaten with things you built | The Siege of Thimbleton (3 waves). Gnarlgrim can only be *Tangled* by the **Bobbin Ballistas** you build and fire |\n35\t| Teleport portals between areas | **Bobbin Portals** |\n36\t| Punny, warm, earnest localisation with quirky dialects | The same tone (§7.10, §6.15) |\n37\t| Online co-op building (DQB2) | Co-op **Quilting Bee** rounds in AI Hub sessions |\n38\t\n39\t---\n40\t\n41\t## 1. Title selection\n42\t\n43\tTASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, picked by a real RNG from 50 silently brainstormed titles. Fifty riffs were written. None uses the trademark \"Dragon Quest\" verbatim. Examples of the kinds of riff: Wagon Quest Builders, Dragon Crest Bricklayers, Dragon Quarry Builders. One was picked with Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which draws from OS entropy (`os.urandom`), not a seeded PRNG. Result: index 42, **\"Dragon Quilt Builders\"**.\n44\t\n45\tThe pick sets the whole art and writing direction: the world is a patchwork quilt. Every block is a fabric patch with stitched seams, the villain unravels things, tools are sewing tools, and puns are sewing puns.\n46\t\n47\tFile naming (fixed; do not improvise): GameTitle = `DragonQuiltBuilders`, SOURCE = `DragonQuestBuilders`.\n48\t- Dev folder: `./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740/`\n49\t- Deliverable: `./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html`\n50\t- Exit interview: `./exit-interview.json`\n51\t\n52\tOn-screen title (logo, `<title>`, About screen, AI Hub name): **Dragon Quilt Builders**.\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 2. Design pillars\n57\t\n58\t1. **Everything is a patch.** Each block is a 16-unit fabric square with stitched edges. Building feels like quilting: colourful, cosy and tactile. Breaking a block makes a satisfying *rrrip* and a burst of fabric scraps. Placing one makes a *stitch!* and a squash-in.\n59\t2. **You are the only one with the Knack.** NPCs cannot build. Every bed, door and lantern you make visibly changes their lives: they use your rooms, sleep, eat, cook, help, and thank you. Make that cause and effect loud: popups, hearts, jingles.\n60\t3. **Readable at ten feet.** Big chunky pixels (`res: 'ps1'` scaled to the TV), a few large UI elements, controller-first prompts, never dense web-style screens.\n61\t4. **Warm, punny, earnest.** Dragon Quest-style writing: short lines, exclamation marks, gentle jokes, distinct voices, and a silent hero.\n62\t5. **Data-driven and designer-ready.** Blocks, items, recipes, rooms, monsters, residents, quests, waves, songs and world stamps are all plain data tables. The building mechanic doubles as the level editor: dev mode exports any region you build as a stamp.\n63\t\n64\t---\n65\t\n66\t## 3. Deliverables, folder layout, commands\n67\t\n68\t```\n69\t./PLAN.md, ./TASK.md, ./STAGE.md                      (given)\n70\t./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html   (built; the game)\n71\t./exit-interview.json                                  (written at the end; parses as JSON)\n72\t./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740/\n73\t    engine/            copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff v0.85.2 (my-3d2dge.js is modified, see §4.2)\n74\t    vendor/aihub/      session.js (unmodified client 1.4.0), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (testing only)\n75\t    src/               game modules, concatenated in file-name order (see §5.2)\n76\t    src/index.template.html\n77\t    tools/build.mjs    bundles everything into the root HTML; also writes dev/index.html\n78\t    tools/hub-serve.mjs  serves a folder on 127.0.0.1:<OS port> for the hub harness (prints the port; kills only its own PID)\n79\t    tests/run.mjs      THE test command (see §11)\n80\t    tests/lib/*.mjs    headless loader, fake hub bus, autopilot\n81\t    tests/unit/*.test.mjs, tests/play/*.test.mjs, tests/net/*.test.mjs, tests/browser/*.mjs\n82\t    dev/index.html     loads engine + src files separately (debugging, file://)\n83\t    check-output/      screenshots and filmstrips (git-ignored)\n84\t```\n85\t\n86\tGetting the dependencies:\n87\t- Do not clone into `/tmp`, which is a small shared tmpfs and nearly full. Work inside the dev folder.\n88\t- Engine:\n89\t  ```\n90\t  git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git _eng\n91\t  cd _eng\n92\t  git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n93\t  ```\n94\t  Then copy `dist/handoff/*` into `engine/` and delete `_eng`.\n95\t- Hub: `curl -fsSO` the four files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/` (session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js) into `vendor/aihub/`. Read `lib/hub/README.md` (the protocol) once, whole.\n96\t- Inside `engine/`, run `npm install` (Playwright 1.63.0 is pinned) and `npx playwright install chromium`. Both are for the browser checks only.\n97\t\n98\tCommands (run from the dev folder):\n99\t\n100\t| Command | What it does |\n101\t|---|---|\n102\t| `node tools/build.mjs` | Writes the root HTML: `<meta charset>`, then **session.js inlined unmodified as the first script in `<head>`**, the hub meta tags, the boot splash, the engine, then `src/*.js` in order. Also writes `dev/index.html`. Fails on a missing file or a syntax error (`node --check` on each module first). |\n103\t| `node tests/run.mjs` | Every headless suite. Prints **only failures** and one final line: `N passed, M failed (12.3 s)`. Exit code 1 on failure. |\n104\t| `node tests/run.mjs --only voxel,rooms` | A subset (rerun only what the change covers). |\n105\t| `node tests/run.mjs --browser` | Adds the Playwright suites: the AI Hub harness checks and the screenshot capture. Still one line of output plus failures. |\n106\t| `node engine/tools/check.mjs <html>#<scene>` | The engine's browser checker (errors, look notes, snapshot). |\n107\t| `node engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs <html>#play --steps \"...\" --seed 1` | Frame-by-frame strip, for judging animations. |\n108\t\n109\tPorts and processes: `tools/hub-serve.mjs` runs `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` and parses the printed port. It fetches the game page and checks that its `<title>` contains \"Dragon Quilt Builders\" before trusting anything. It kills only the PID it spawned. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. Prefer `file://` and headless Node everywhere else.\n110\t\n111\t---\n112\t\n113\t## 4. Tech stack\n114\t\n115\t### 4.1 Stack\n116\t- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2, full engine** (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`, which already contains every module: story, grid, songs, ...). Plain ES2020 JavaScript, no framework, no bundler beyond `tools/build.mjs` (string concatenation).\n117\t- **Canvas 2D pixel rendering** through the engine's renderer. `res: 'ps1'` (320×240) with `expand: true`, so a 16:9 TV shows about 427×240 game pixels at a whole-number scale. GPU lighting is not used (it requires a TileMap); canvas lighting is.\n118\t- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth and music sequencer, plus our own Web Audio formant voice synth (§6.17) on the engine's `AudioContext`.\n119\t- **AI Hub Session Protocol v1**, client 1.4.0, shared-world mode (§6.21).\n120\t- **Node 18+** for headless tests (`vm` contexts, as `engine/game/test.mjs` does). **Playwright** (pinned by the handoff) for the browser checks.\n121\t- No external runtime dependencies and no CDN. The HTML must work from `file://`.\n122\t- **Performance:** the budget is unlimited (TASK.md), so add detail, lights and effects freely. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope. The atlas and culling design in §6.1 exists for correctness and a smooth feel, not as an optimisation target.\n123\t\n124\t### 4.2 Engine changes (keep a list; it goes into the About screen and the exit interview)\n125\tEdit `engine/my-3d2dge.js` directly, with a `// DQB:` comment on every change:\n126\t1. **Input:** an `input.wheelSteps()` accessor (whole notches this step, from `deltaY`) that works while `camera.wheel` is `false`, so the wheel can cycle the hotbar. A right-stick flick helper, `input.flick('x')`, which returns -1/0/1 once per push past 0.6 with a 0.25 s rearm.\n127\t2. **Dialog:** an `onLetter(ch, index, page)` hook called for each newly shown character (default: the old single blip). Our voice synth uses it per speaker.\n128\t3. **Audio:** a third bus, `audio._voiceBus` (master → voice), with `audio.voiceVolume` and a standard setting, \"Voices\" (0–100%).\n129\t4. **Humanoid weapons:** new weapon kinds `'stick'`, `'club'` and `'ripper'`, drawn next to the existing `'sword'`/`'staff'` code using the same blade/tip joints, so `rig.tip()`, smears and moves keep working.\n130\t5. **Glyph default:** `input.glyphs` defaults to `'playstation'` for this game. Our Settings item \"Button glyphs\" (PlayStation / Xbox) writes it.\n131\t6. **Body ceilings:** none in the engine; `VoxelBody` (§6.1.6) handles them in game code. If the renderer, Body or Input need anything else, add it the same way and log it.\n132\t\n133\tDo **not** rebuild the standard features: pause menu, settings, rebinding, full screen, boot screen, save slots. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })` and `game.saves`.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## 5. Architecture\n138\t\n139\t### 5.1 Principles\n140\t- **One namespace.** `globalThis.DQ` holds every module. Each `src/NN-name.js` is an IIFE that adds to `DQ` and reads only what earlier files defined.\n141\t- **Logic and drawing are split.** Every system has `update(dt)` with no rendering, and a separate `draw(r)`. All logic runs headless in Node: `new E.Game()` is headless without a DOM and `draw` is skipped. UI screens are state machines (focus index, open tab, scroll), driven by input actions and testable headless; drawing reads that state.\n142\t- **Determinism.** No `Math.random` in game logic. Use named seeded streams, `DQ.rng(label)` (built on `E.rng(seed ^ hash(label))`). World seed `20261103`. In a hub session, shared streams come from `AIHub.rng(label)`. Cosmetic randomness (particles, weather) uses its own streams.\n143\t- **Event bus.** `DQ.events.on(name, fn)` and `emit(name, payload)` decouple systems. Quests, tutorials, the HUD, audio stingers, stats and tests all listen. Events:\n144\t  - Blocks and items: `blockBroken`, `blockPlaced`, `objectPlaced`, `objectRemoved`, `itemGained`, `itemUsed`, `crafted`, `notion`\n145\t  - Town: `roomFormed`, `roomLost`, `cheer`, `townLevel`, `patternPlaced`, `patternDone`\n146\t  - Residents and combat: `npcArrived`, `talk`, `monsterKilled`, `builderHurt`, `builderKO`\n147\t  - Story and time: `questStep`, `questDone`, `timeOfDay`, `siegeWave`, `bossPhase`, `chapterClear`\n148\t- **One context object.** `DQ.ctx = { game, world, builder, town, story, quests, npcs, monsters, ui, audioX, tuning, events, net, mode }` is created per play session. Systems receive it in their constructor and never reach for globals beyond `DQ`.\n149\t- **Data tables** live in `src/0x-data-*.js` as JSON-compatible literals: no functions in data except clearly marked `make` generators for stamps. Designers edit those files.\n150\t\n151\t### 5.2 Modules (`src/`, concatenated in this order)\n152\t\n153\t| File | Owns | Key API |\n154\t|---|---|---|\n155\t| `00-core.js` | `DQ` namespace, constants (`T=16`, world sizes), `hash`, `rng`, event bus, `DQ.ctx` factory, logging | `DQ.rng(label)`, `DQ.events` |\n156\t| `01-data-blocks.js` | Block registry (§7.2) | `DQ.BLOCKS[id]`, `DQ.blockId(key)` |\n157\t| `02-data-items.js` | Items, recipes, notions (§7.3–7.4) | `DQ.ITEMS`, `DQ.RECIPES` |\n158\t| `03-data-objects.js` | Placeable objects and their `E.SHAPES` entries (§7.5) | `DQ.OBJECTS` |\n159\t| `04-data-rooms.js` | Room recipes, town levels (§7.6–7.7) | `DQ.ROOMS`, `DQ.LEVELS` |\n160\t| `05-data-world.js` | Regions, stamps (ASCII layers), POIs, portals, plant scatter, spawn tables (§7.1) | `DQ.WORLD` |\n161\t| `06-data-npcs.js` | Residents: look, voice, schedule, job, dialog nodes (§7.8) | `DQ.NPCS` |\n162\t| `07-data-monsters.js` | Monster and boss stats, AI params, drops (§7.9) | `DQ.MONSTERS` |\n163\t| `08-data-quests.js` | Quests, steps, conditions, rewards, `E.Story` nodes, cutscene ids (§7.10) | `DQ.QUESTS`, `DQ.STORY_NODES` |\n164\t| `09-data-audio.js` | Songs, jingles, sfx voices, voice profiles (§7.11–7.12) | `DQ.SONGS`, `DQ.SFX`, `DQ.VOICES` |\n165\t| `09b-data-tuning.js` | Tuning defaults and slider metadata (§6.19) | `DQ.TUNING_DEFS` |\n166\t| `10-voxel-world.js` | Voxel storage, edits, queries, collision, raycast, save diff | `DQ.VoxelWorld` (§6.1) |\n167\t| `11-voxel-render.js` | Face-sprite atlas, column-run rendering, cutaway, water, void fringe, overlays | `DQ.VoxelRenderer` |\n168\t| `12-worldgen.js` | Terrain, layers, ores, trees, water, stamps, plants, portals | `DQ.generateWorld(world, def)` |\n169\t| `13-physics.js` | `VoxelBody` (ceilings, auto-hop, water, void), stair ramps | `DQ.VoxelBody` |\n170\t| `14-input.js` | Action presets, intents, cursor modes, mouse picking, glyph icons and prompt strings | `DQ.Controls`, `DQ.Glyphs` |\n171\t| `15-builder.js` | Player entity: stats, equipment, actions (swing, break, place, use, eat, interact), KO and respawn | `DQ.Builder` |\n172\t| `16-inventory.js` | Bag, hotbar, chests, Town Chest, equipment, stacks, overflow | `DQ.Bag`, `DQ.Chest` |\n173\t| `17-crafting.js` | Stations, recipe availability, crafting, notion unlocks | `DQ.Crafting` |\n174\t| `18-objects.js` | Object instances: doors, lights, beds, stations, traps, ballistas, portals, plants (regrowth) | `DQ.Objects` |\n175\t| `19-rooms.js` | Room detection and classification, room registry | `DQ.Rooms` |\n176\t| `20-town.js` | Banner, border, Cheer, town level, Patterns (ghosts, progress, helpers) | `DQ.Town` |\n177\t| `21-nav.js` | Voxel navigation graph, A*, flow fields, breaker paths | `DQ.Nav` |\n178\t| `22-npcs.js` | Resident AI: schedule, needs, jobs, helping, fighting, Cheer bubbles | `DQ.Residents` |\n179\t| `23-monsters.js` | Monster AI, spawning, despawning | `DQ.Monsters` |\n180\t| `24-combat.js` | Damage, hit effects, knockback, i-frames, drops, the Lost Bundle | `DQ.Combat`, `DQ.Drops` |\n181\t| `25-siege.js` | Siege director (prep, waves, fail/retry), boss directors | `DQ.Siege` |\n182\t| `26-bosses.js` | Sir Buttonsworth, Gnarlgrim, Tangle mechanics | `DQ.Bosses` |\n183\t| `27-time.js` | Day clock, lighting by hour, weather, sleep | `DQ.Clock` |\n184\t| `28-story.js` | Quest director (steps on events), `E.Story` wrapper, cutscene scripts, objective targets | `DQ.Quests`, `DQ.Cutscenes` |\n185\t| `29-ui-hud.js` | HUD, prompts, popups (Notion, Room, Level), Cheer, damage numbers, objective marker, cursor | `DQ.Hud` |\n186\t| `30-ui-menus.js` | Title, name entry, Satchel (Bag/Map/Journal), crafting, chest, travel, banner, dialog skin, results, credits, About | `DQ.Menus` |\n187\t| `31-audio.js` | Music director (per region, time and town level layers), sfx defs, Animalese voice synth | `DQ.AudioX` |\n188\t| `32-save.js` | `game.saves` read/write, versioning, migrate, autosave | `DQ.Save` |\n189\t| `33-dev.js` | Dev mode panel, cheats, sliders, overlays, stamp export | `DQ.Dev` |\n190\t| `34-attract.js` | Attract-mode director (vignettes, AI builder, cheats) | `DQ.Attract` |\n191\t| `35-ai.js` | Shared AI \"autopilot\" (walkTo, breakAt, placeAt, craft, talk, fight). Used by attract, tests and Bee bots | `DQ.Autopilot` |\n192\t| `36-net.js` | AI Hub session flow, message codec, host and client logic, loopback | `DQ.Net` |\n193\t| `37-bee.js` | Quilting Bee rules (hub round and solo challenge), its world, scoring | `DQ.Bee` |\n194\t| `38-scenes.js` | Scenes: title, attract, newgame, intro, play, results, credits, bee, hubcard | `DQ.Scenes` |\n195\t| `99-main.js` | Creates `E.Game`, settings, menu, boot steps, `game.debug`, starts | — |\n196\t\n197\t### 5.3 Game object and scenes\n198\t```js\n199\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter'],\n200\t  input: DQ.Controls.PLAY, id: 'dragon-quilt-builders',\n201\t  menu: { saves: { slots: 3, version: 1, migrate: DQ.Save.migrate },\n202\t          items: [['Satchel', openSatchel], ['Dev Tools', DQ.Dev.open], ['About & Credits', openAbout]],   // Dev Tools hidden in a hub session\n203\t          settings: DQ.SETTINGS, controls: DQ.Controls.NAMES, quit: 'title' } });\n204\t```\n205\t- **Scenes:** `title` (menu and attract countdown), `attract`, `newgame` (name, look), `intro` (story crawl), `play` (`pausable: true`, `camera: { zoom: false }`: the engine's continuous player zoom stays off, because our own zoom steps call `game.setZoom`, §6.1.3), `results` (chapter clear), `credits`, `about` (also openable over the pause menu), `bee` (Quilting Bee, solo or hub), `hubcard` (the game's own AI Hub card).\n206\t- **Boot steps** (`game.start({ boot: { title: 'DRAGON QUILT BUILDERS', steps } })`):\n207\t  1. \"Unrolling the quilt\" (world generation)\n208\t  2. \"Threading the needles\" (face-sprite atlas, icons)\n209\t  3. \"Waking the residents\" (nav graph)\n210\t  4. \"Tuning the voices\" (audio definitions)\n211\t\n212\t  Boot messages: \"Counting the buttons\", \"Untangling yarn\", \"Ironing the seams\".\n213\t- **`play.update(dt)` order:**\n214\t  1. `Controls.intent()`\n215\t  2. `Builder.update`\n216\t  3. block actions\n217\t  4. `Objects.update` (doors, traps, ballistas, plants)\n218\t  5. `Residents.update`\n219\t  6. `Monsters.update`\n220\t  7. `Combat.resolve`\n221\t  8. `Drops.update`\n222\t  9. `Town.update` (rooms debounce, Cheer, Patterns)\n223\t  10. `Quests.update`\n224\t  11. `Clock.update` (spawns, weather)\n225\t  12. `Siege.update`\n226\t  13. camera (`game.focus` on the builder with lead)\n227\t  14. `Hud.update`\n228\t\n229\t  A modal UI (dialog, menu, cutscene) short-circuits gameplay updates. The world still animates (particles, water) and the clock pauses.\n230\t- **`play.draw(r)` order:**\n231\t  1. void background\n232\t  2. `VoxelRenderer.queue(r)` (sets `r._map`)\n233\t  3. objects (`r.shape`)\n234\t  4. Pattern ghosts\n235\t  5. town border stitches\n236\t  6. drops\n237\t  7. actors (builder with `xray`, residents, monsters)\n238\t  8. cursor\n239\t  9. lights (`game.lights.add`)\n240\t  10. `r.overlay` (HUD, popups, dialog, menus)\n241\t- **`game.debug`:**\n242\t  - `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, day, hour, builder: {x,y,z,hp,maxHp,belly,equip}, bag: {id:n}, town: {level, cheer, border, rooms:[type]}, quests: {id: step|'done'}, residents: [{id,x,y,state}], monsters: n, siege: {phase, wave}, boss: {id, hp, phase}, errors: n }`.\n243\t  - Helpers: `teleport(x,y,z)`, `give(id,n)`, `flag(k,v)`, `beat(id)` (jump to a story beat), `world` (the VoxelWorld), `ctx`.\n244\t\n245\t---\n246\t## 6. Systems\n247\t\n248\t### 6.1 Voxel world (the riskiest system; built first, see §9 MS1)\n249\t\n250\tThe engine's `TileMap` is a heightmap: one wall type per cell, no passages under things. Building needs real 3D blocks (roofs, bridges, tunnels, mixed-material walls), so the game has its own `VoxelWorld`. It plugs into the engine where the engine expects a map."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"d8792dc8-2bfc-48df-aeb4-697c8d566583","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:59:24.962Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-215911__x5b1962/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Dragon Quilt Builders\n\n*A build plan for an original block-building action RPG inspired by Dragon Quest Builders.*\nWritten for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from `TASK.md` and this file alone.\nPlan date: 2026-11-03. Engine: my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit `4390a98`). AI Hub client: session.js 1.4.0 (ai-hub commit `f8835e48`).\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Title** | **Dragon Quilt Builders** (picked by RNG; see §1) |\n| **Pitch** | The patchwork realm came apart at the seams when the dragon **Frayvern** pulled out its Golden Thread and swallowed everyone's *Knack*, the gift of making things. You are the last **Builder**. Break blocks, gather materials, get *Notions* (ideas for recipes), craft, and build rooms block by block. Win the hearts of quirky residents, raise the town of **Thimbleton**, defend it from a monster siege, and unpick the knotted boss **Gnarlgrim**. |\n| **Genre** | Sandbox action RPG with block building, crafting and town building. Same family as the source: real-time combat and a story told in chapters. |\n| **Perspective** | Third-person **isometric 2.5D** over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90° steps (Q/E or the right stick) and zooms. **Three-quarter** is an optional view in Settings. Characters are the engine's 3D puppet rigs drawn as pixel art. Why iso: it shows two side faces and the top of every block, so 3D building reads clearly with a fixed camera, and it is the engine's native strength. |\n| **Core loop** | Explore → break blocks and gather → get a Notion → craft at a station → build rooms and Patterns → residents move in and give Cheer → town levels up and unlocks more → the story pushes you into harder regions and finally a siege and a boss. |\n| **Demo scope** | \"Chapter One: A Stitch in Time\", about 60–75 minutes for a new player. It has 5 residents, 6 regions, 8 room types, 30+ block and object types, 17 quests, a mini-boss, a siege with 3 waves and a final boss. After the credits, free play continues. A co-op **Quilting Bee** round (AI Hub shared world, 1–4 players) is also playable solo from the title. |\n| **Deliverable** | One self-contained HTML file in this folder's root: `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html`. Dev files live in the subfolder of the same name. `exit-interview.json` sits beside the HTML. |\n\nThe source's distinctive elements, and what each becomes here (all names, characters, art, music and places are original):\n\n| Dragon Quest Builders | Dragon Quilt Builders |\n|---|---|\n| The dark lord took the power to build; you are the legendary Builder | Frayvern swallowed the *Knack*; only you can still make things. NPCs marvel at beds and doors (\"You *made* that? On purpose?!\") |\n| A goddess guides you as a voice from the sky (Rubiss) | **Granny Gossamer**, the Great Quilter who stitched the world: a warm, scatterbrained voice in golden dialog boxes |\n| Banner of Hope founds a base; base level rises | **Banner of Mending**: planting it stitches a glowing border on the ground. **Cheer** points raise the **Town Level** (1–5), which widens the border and unlocks recipes |\n| Inspiration: touching new materials gives recipe ideas | **Notions** (\"You've got a notion!\"; *notions* is the sewing-shop word for haberdashery) |\n| Rooms recognised from walls ≥2 high, a door and a light, then typed by their furniture | Same rule. 8 room recipes (Bedroom, Workshop, Kitchen, Mess Hall, Smithy, Guard Post, Snug, Quilters' Hall) |\n| Blueprints that residents help build | **Patterns**: ghost-block layouts. Residents take materials from the Town Chest and place blocks too |\n| Resident requests (the \"!\" over their heads), gratitude hearts | Requests with \"!\" balloons. **Cheer bubbles** (hearts) float over happy residents; walk through them to collect |\n| Hunger, day/night, sleeping in beds | **Belly** meter, a 12-minute day, sleep through the night in a bed |\n| Weapons double as block-breaking tools with tiers | Darning Stick → Cobble Club → Copper Bodkin → **Seam Ripper**, the only tool that cuts Black Knots |\n| Base-defence sieges; bosses beaten with things you built | The Siege of Thimbleton (3 waves). Gnarlgrim can only be *Tangled* by the **Bobbin Ballistas** you build and fire |\n| Teleport portals between areas | **Bobbin Portals** |\n| Punny, warm, earnest localisation with quirky dialects | The same tone (§7.10, §6.15) |\n| Online co-op building (DQB2) | Co-op **Quilting Bee** rounds in AI Hub sessions |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Title selection\n\nTASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, picked by a real RNG from 50 silently brainstormed titles. Fifty riffs were written. None uses the trademark \"Dragon Quest\" verbatim. Examples of the kinds of riff: Wagon Quest Builders, Dragon Crest Bricklayers, Dragon Quarry Builders. One was picked with Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which draws from OS entropy (`os.urandom`), not a seeded PRNG. Result: index 42, **\"Dragon Quilt Builders\"**.\n\nThe pick sets the whole art and writing direction: the world is a patchwork quilt. Every block is a fabric patch with stitched seams, the villain unravels things, tools are sewing tools, and puns are sewing puns.\n\nFile naming (fixed; do not improvise): GameTitle = `DragonQuiltBuilders`, SOURCE = `DragonQuestBuilders`.\n- Dev folder: `./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740/`\n- Deliverable: `./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html`\n- Exit interview: `./exit-interview.json`\n\nOn-screen title (logo, `<title>`, About screen, AI Hub name): **Dragon Quilt Builders**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Design pillars\n\n1. **Everything is a patch.** Each block is a 16-unit fabric square with stitched edges. Building feels like quilting: colourful, cosy and tactile. Breaking a block makes a satisfying *rrrip* and a burst of fabric scraps. Placing one makes a *stitch!* and a squash-in.\n2. **You are the only one with the Knack.** NPCs cannot build. Every bed, door and lantern you make visibly changes their lives: they use your rooms, sleep, eat, cook, help, and thank you. Make that cause and effect loud: popups, hearts, jingles.\n3. **Readable at ten feet.** Big chunky pixels (`res: 'ps1'` scaled to the TV), a few large UI elements, controller-first prompts, never dense web-style screens.\n4. **Warm, punny, earnest.** Dragon Quest-style writing: short lines, exclamation marks, gentle jokes, distinct voices, and a silent hero.\n5. **Data-driven and designer-ready.** Blocks, items, recipes, rooms, monsters, residents, quests, waves, songs and world stamps are all plain data tables. The building mechanic doubles as the level editor: dev mode exports any region you build as a stamp.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deliverables, folder layout, commands\n\n```\n./PLAN.md, ./TASK.md, ./STAGE.md                      (given)\n./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740.html   (built; the game)\n./exit-interview.json                                  (written at the end; parses as JSON)\n./DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174740/\n    engine/            copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff v0.85.2 (my-3d2dge.js is modified, see §4.2)\n    vendor/aihub/      session.js (unmodified client 1.4.0), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (testing only)\n    src/               game modules, concatenated in file-name order (see §5.2)\n    src/index.template.html\n    tools/build.mjs    bundles everything into the root HTML; also writes dev/index.html\n    tools/hub-serve.mjs  serves a folder on 127.0.0.1:<OS port> for the hub harness (prints the port; kills only its own PID)\n    tests/run.mjs      THE test command (see §11)\n    tests/lib/*.mjs    headless loader, fake hub bus, autopilot\n    tests/unit/*.test.mjs, tests/play/*.test.mjs, tests/net/*.test.mjs, tests/browser/*.mjs\n    dev/index.html     loads engine + src files separately (debugging, file://)\n    check-output/      screenshots and filmstrips (git-ignored)\n```\n\nGetting the dependencies:\n- Do not clone into `/tmp`, which is a small shared tmpfs and nearly full. Work inside the dev folder.\n- Engine:\n  ```\n  git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git _eng\n  cd _eng\n  git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n  ```\n  Then copy `dist/handoff/*` into `engine/` and delete `_eng`.\n- Hub: `curl -fsSO` the four files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/` (session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js) into `vendor/aihub/`. Read `lib/hub/README.md` (the protocol) once, whole.\n- Inside `engine/`, run `npm install` (Playwright 1.63.0 is pinned) and `npx playwright install chromium`. Both are for the browser checks only.\n\nCommands (run from the dev folder):\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `node tools/build.mjs` | Writes the root HTML: `<meta charset>`, then **session.js inlined unmodified as the first script in `<head>`**, the hub meta tags, the boot splash, the engine, then `src/*.js` in order. Also writes `dev/index.html`. Fails on a missing file or a syntax error (`node --check` on each module first). |\n| `node tests/run.mjs` | Every headless suite. Prints **only failures** and one final line: `N passed, M failed (12.3 s)`. Exit code 1 on failure. |\n| `node tests/run.mjs --only voxel,rooms` | A subset (rerun only what the change covers). |\n| `node tests/run.mjs --browser` | Adds the Playwright suites: the AI Hub harness checks and the screenshot capture. Still one line of output plus failures. |\n| `node engine/tools/check.mjs <html>#<scene>` | The engine's browser checker (errors, look notes, snapshot). |\n| `node engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs <html>#play --steps \"...\" --seed 1` | Frame-by-frame strip, for judging animations. |\n\nPorts and processes: `tools/hub-serve.mjs` runs `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` and parses the printed port. It fetches the game page and checks that its `<title>` contains \"Dragon Quilt Builders\" before trusting anything. It kills only the PID it spawned. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`. Prefer `file://` and headless Node everywhere else.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tech stack\n\n### 4.1 Stack\n- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2, full engine** (`engine/my-3d2dge.js`, which already contains every module: story, grid, songs, ...). Plain ES2020 JavaScript, no framework, no bundler beyond `tools/build.mjs` (string concatenation).\n- **Canvas 2D pixel rendering** through the engine's renderer. `res: 'ps1'` (320×240) with `expand: true`, so a 16:9 TV shows about 427×240 game pixels at a whole-number scale. GPU lighting is not used (it requires a TileMap); canvas lighting is.\n- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth and music sequencer, plus our own Web Audio formant voice synth (§6.17) on the engine's `AudioContext`.\n- **AI Hub Session Protocol v1**, client 1.4.0, shared-world mode (§6.21).\n- **Node 18+** for headless tests (`vm` contexts, as `engine/game/test.mjs` does). **Playwright** (pinned by the handoff) for the browser checks.\n- No external runtime dependencies and no CDN. The HTML must work from `file://`.\n- **Performance:** the budget is unlimited (TASK.md), so add detail, lights and effects freely. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope. The atlas and culling design in §6.1 exists for correctness and a smooth feel, not as an optimisation target.\n\n### 4.2 Engine changes (keep a list; it goes into the About screen and the exit interview)\nEdit `engine/my-3d2dge.js` directly, with a `// DQB:` comment on every change:\n1. **Input:** an `input.wheelSteps()` accessor (whole notches this step, from `deltaY`) that works while `camera.wheel` is `false`, so the wheel can cycle the hotbar. A right-stick flick helper, `input.flick('x')`, which returns -1/0/1 once per push past 0.6 with a 0.25 s rearm.\n2. **Dialog:** an `onLetter(ch, index, page)` hook called for each newly shown character (default: the old single blip). Our voice synth uses it per speaker.\n3. **Audio:** a third bus, `audio._voiceBus` (master → voice), with `audio.voiceVolume` and a standard setting, \"Voices\" (0–100%).\n4. **Humanoid weapons:** new weapon kinds `'stick'`, `'club'` and `'ripper'`, drawn next to the existing `'sword'`/`'staff'` code using the same blade/tip joints, so `rig.tip()`, smears and moves keep working.\n5. **Glyph default:** `input.glyphs` defaults to `'playstation'` for this game. Our Settings item \"Button glyphs\" (PlayStation / Xbox) writes it.\n6. **Body ceilings:** none in the engine; `VoxelBody` (§6.1.6) handles them in game code. If the renderer, Body or Input need anything else, add it the same way and log it.\n\nDo **not** rebuild the standard features: pause menu, settings, rebinding, full screen, boot screen, save slots. Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })` and `game.saves`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Architecture\n\n### 5.1 Principles\n- **One namespace.** `globalThis.DQ` holds every module. Each `src/NN-name.js` is an IIFE that adds to `DQ` and reads only what earlier files defined.\n- **Logic and drawing are split.** Every system has `update(dt)` with no rendering, and a separate `draw(r)`. All logic runs headless in Node: `new E.Game()` is headless without a DOM and `draw` is skipped. UI screens are state machines (focus index, open tab, scroll), driven by input actions and testable headless; drawing reads that state.\n- **Determinism.** No `Math.random` in game logic. Use named seeded streams, `DQ.rng(label)` (built on `E.rng(seed ^ hash(label))`). World seed `20261103`. In a hub session, shared streams come from `AIHub.rng(label)`. Cosmetic randomness (particles, weather) uses its own streams.\n- **Event bus.** `DQ.events.on(name, fn)` and `emit(name, payload)` decouple systems. Quests, tutorials, the HUD, audio stingers, stats and tests all listen. Events:\n  - Blocks and items: `blockBroken`, `blockPlaced`, `objectPlaced`, `objectRemoved`, `itemGained`, `itemUsed`, `crafted`, `notion`\n  - Town: `roomFormed`, `roomLost`, `cheer`, `townLevel`, `patternPlaced`, `patternDone`\n  - Residents and combat: `npcArrived`, `talk`, `monsterKilled`, `builderHurt`, `builderKO`\n  - Story and time: `questStep`, `questDone`, `timeOfDay`, `siegeWave`, `bossPhase`, `chapterClear`\n- **One context object.** `DQ.ctx = { game, world, builder, town, story, quests, npcs, monsters, ui, audioX, tuning, events, net, mode }` is created per play session. Systems receive it in their constructor and never reach for globals beyond `DQ`.\n- **Data tables** live in `src/0x-data-*.js` as JSON-compatible literals: no functions in data except clearly marked `make` generators for stamps. Designers edit those files.\n\n### 5.2 Modules (`src/`, concatenated in this order)\n\n| File | Owns | Key API |\n|---|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `DQ` namespace, constants (`T=16`, world sizes), `hash`, `rng`, event bus, `DQ.ctx` factory, logging | `DQ.rng(label)`, `DQ.events` |\n| `01-data-blocks.js` | Block registry (§7.2) | `DQ.BLOCKS[id]`, `DQ.blockId(key)` |\n| `02-data-items.js` | Items, recipes, notions (§7.3–7.4) | `DQ.ITEMS`, `DQ.RECIPES` |\n| `03-data-objects.js` | Placeable objects and their `E.SHAPES` entries (§7.5) | `DQ.OBJECTS` |\n| `04-data-rooms.js` | Room recipes, town levels (§7.6–7.7) | `DQ.ROOMS`, `DQ.LEVELS` |\n| `05-data-world.js` | Regions, stamps (ASCII layers), POIs, portals, plant scatter, spawn tables (§7.1) | `DQ.WORLD` |\n| `06-data-npcs.js` | Residents: look, voice, schedule, job, dialog nodes (§7.8) | `DQ.NPCS` |\n| `07-data-monsters.js` | Monster and boss stats, AI params, drops (§7.9) | `DQ.MONSTERS` |\n| `08-data-quests.js` | Quests, steps, conditions, rewards, `E.Story` nodes, cutscene ids (§7.10) | `DQ.QUESTS`, `DQ.STORY_NODES` |\n| `09-data-audio.js` | Songs, jingles, sfx voices, voice profiles (§7.11–7.12) | `DQ.SONGS`, `DQ.SFX`, `DQ.VOICES` |\n| `09b-data-tuning.js` | Tuning defaults and slider metadata (§6.19) | `DQ.TUNING_DEFS` |\n| `10-voxel-world.js` | Voxel storage, edits, queries, collision, raycast, save diff | `DQ.VoxelWorld` (§6.1) |\n| `11-voxel-render.js` | Face-sprite atlas, column-run rendering, cutaway, water, void fringe, overlays | `DQ.VoxelRenderer` |\n| `12-worldgen.js` | Terrain, layers, ores, trees, water, stamps, plants, portals | `DQ.generateWorld(world, def)` |\n| `13-physics.js` | `VoxelBody` (ceilings, auto-hop, water, void), stair ramps | `DQ.VoxelBody` |\n| `14-input.js` | Action presets, intents, cursor modes, mouse picking, glyph icons and prompt strings | `DQ.Controls`, `DQ.Glyphs` |\n| `15-builder.js` | Player entity: stats, equipment, actions (swing, break, place, use, eat, interact), KO and respawn | `DQ.Builder` |\n| `16-inventory.js` | Bag, hotbar, chests, Town Chest, equipment, stacks, overflow | `DQ.Bag`, `DQ.Chest` |\n| `17-crafting.js` | Stations, recipe availability, crafting, notion unlocks | `DQ.Crafting` |\n| `18-objects.js` | Object instances: doors, lights, beds, stations, traps, ballistas, portals, plants (regrowth) | `DQ.Objects` |\n| `19-rooms.js` | Room detection and classification, room registry | `DQ.Rooms` |\n| `20-town.js` | Banner, border, Cheer, town level, Patterns (ghosts, progress, helpers) | `DQ.Town` |\n| `21-nav.js` | Voxel navigation graph, A*, flow fields, breaker paths | `DQ.Nav` |\n| `22-npcs.js` | Resident AI: schedule, needs, jobs, helping, fighting, Cheer bubbles | `DQ.Residents` |\n| `23-monsters.js` | Monster AI, spawning, despawning | `DQ.Monsters` |\n| `24-combat.js` | Damage, hit effects, knockback, i-frames, drops, the Lost Bundle | `DQ.Combat`, `DQ.Drops` |\n| `25-siege.js` | Siege director (prep, waves, fail/retry), boss directors | `DQ.Siege` |\n| `26-bosses.js` | Sir Buttonsworth, Gnarlgrim, Tangle mechanics | `DQ.Bosses` |\n| `27-time.js` | Day clock, lighting by hour, weather, sleep | `DQ.Clock` |\n| `28-story.js` | Quest director (steps on events), `E.Story` wrapper, cutscene scripts, objective targets | `DQ.Quests`, `DQ.Cutscenes` |\n| `29-ui-hud.js` | HUD, prompts, popups (Notion, Room, Level), Cheer, damage numbers, objective marker, cursor | `DQ.Hud` |\n| `30-ui-menus.js` | Title, name entry, Satchel (Bag/Map/Journal), crafting, chest, travel, banner, dialog skin, results, credits, About | `DQ.Menus` |\n| `31-audio.js` | Music director (per region, time and town level layers), sfx defs, Animalese voice synth | `DQ.AudioX` |\n| `32-save.js` | `game.saves` read/write, versioning, migrate, autosave | `DQ.Save` |\n| `33-dev.js` | Dev mode panel, cheats, sliders, overlays, stamp export | `DQ.Dev` |\n| `34-attract.js` | Attract-mode director (vignettes, AI builder, cheats) | `DQ.Attract` |\n| `35-ai.js` | Shared AI \"autopilot\" (walkTo, breakAt, placeAt, craft, talk, fight). Used by attract, tests and Bee bots | `DQ.Autopilot` |\n| `36-net.js` | AI Hub session flow, message codec, host and client logic, loopback | `DQ.Net` |\n| `37-bee.js` | Quilting Bee rules (hub round and solo challenge), its world, scoring | `DQ.Bee` |\n| `38-scenes.js` | Scenes: title, attract, newgame, intro, play, results, credits, bee, hubcard | `DQ.Scenes` |\n| `99-main.js` | Creates `E.Game`, settings, menu, boot steps, `game.debug`, starts | — |\n\n### 5.3 Game object and scenes\n```js\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter'],\n  input: DQ.Controls.PLAY, id: 'dragon-quilt-builders',\n  menu: { saves: { slots: 3, version: 1, migrate: DQ.Save.migrate },\n          items: [['Satchel', openSatchel], ['Dev Tools', DQ.Dev.open], ['About & Credits', openAbout]],   // Dev Tools hidden in a hub session\n          settings: DQ.SETTINGS, controls: DQ.Controls.NAMES, quit: 'title' } });\n```\n- **Scenes:** `title` (menu and attract countdown), `attract`, `newgame` (name, look), `intro` (story crawl), `play` (`pausable: true`, `camera: { zoom: false }`: the engine's continuous player zoom stays off, because our own zoom steps call `game.setZoom`, §6.1.3), `results` (chapter clear), `credits`, `about` (also openable over the pause menu), `bee` (Quilting Bee, solo or hub), `hubcard` (the game's own AI Hub card).\n- **Boot steps** (`game.start({ boot: { title: 'DRAGON QUILT BUILDERS', steps } })`):\n  1. \"Unrolling the quilt\" (world generation)\n  2. \"Threading the needles\" (face-sprite atlas, icons)\n  3. \"Waking the residents\" (nav graph)\n  4. \"Tuning the voices\" (audio definitions)\n\n  Boot messages: \"Counting the buttons\", \"Untangling yarn\", \"Ironing the seams\".\n- **`play.update(dt)` order:**\n  1. `Controls.intent()`\n  2. `Builder.update`\n  3. block actions\n  4. `Objects.update` (doors, traps, ballistas, plants)\n  5. `Residents.update`\n  6. `Monsters.update`\n  7. `Combat.resolve`\n  8. `Drops.update`\n  9. `Town.update` (rooms debounce, Cheer, Patterns)\n  10. `Quests.update`\n  11. `Clock.update` (spawns, weather)\n  12. `Siege.update`\n  13. camera (`game.focus` on the builder with lead)\n  14. `Hud.update`\n\n  A modal UI (dialog, menu, cutscene) short-circuits gameplay updates. The world still animates (particles, water) and the clock pauses.\n- **`play.draw(r)` order:**\n  1. void background\n  2. `VoxelRenderer.queue(r)` (sets `r._map`)\n  3. objects (`r.shape`)\n  4. Pattern ghosts\n  5. town border stitches\n  6. drops\n  7. actors (builder with `xray`, residents, monsters)\n  8. cursor\n  9. lights (`game.lights.add`)\n  10. `r.overlay` (HUD, popups, dialog, menus)\n- **`game.debug`:**\n  - `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, day, hour, builder: {x,y,z,hp,maxHp,belly,equip}, bag: {id:n}, town: {level, cheer, border, rooms:[type]}, quests: {id: step|'done'}, residents: [{id,x,y,state}], monsters: n, siege: {phase, wave}, boss: {id, hp, phase}, errors: n }`.\n  - Helpers: `teleport(x,y,z)`, `give(id,n)`, `flag(k,v)`, `beat(id)` (jump to a story beat), `world` (the VoxelWorld), `ctx`.\n\n---\n## 6. Systems\n\n### 6.1 Voxel world (the riskiest system; built first, see §9 MS1)\n\nThe engine's `TileMap` is a heightmap: one wall type per cell, no passages under things. Building needs real 3D blocks (roofs, bridges, tunnels, mixed-material walls), so the game has its own `VoxelWorld`. It plugs into the engine where the engine expects a map.","numLines":250,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1675}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QCgFLoHGBtqbrnLdfDQQ6e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
