{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghUmjDcisJoQtDbEKCyH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The final state passes everything, checked by name rather than just a total, and nothing was edited after the final runs.\n\n- **Every check by name:** one run of all 19 headless suites listed 336 named checks, all passing. They cover physics, the builder (Builder's Eye, tool tiers, Spin Stitch, KO and the Lost Bundle) and input glyphs. They also cover items and crafting, objects, rooms, town and Patterns, residents, monsters, the siege and bosses, time, saves, dev tools (including the stamp round trip) and attract mode. The rest are UI and About, the full playthrough, the Bee (win, loss, Master Quilter and Knot Cutter awards) and multiplayer netcode.\n- **The one test command:** `node tests/run.mjs --browser` printed only its count line, \"341 passed, 0 failed\", and exited 0. That run includes the AI Hub harness's four scenarios and the standalone page check.\n- **TODO/FIXME/XXX/placeholder/stub/\"not implemented\":** the search across all 54 files in `src`, `tests` and `tools` printed nothing (exit status 1).\n- **Files:** the game `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-215911.html` and `exit-interview.json` are both in the work folder. The exit interview parses with no empty fields and matches the About screen built into the game.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 21:59:11\n- **Source:** Dragon Quest Builders\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a sandbox action RPG (block building, crafting, town building, real-time combat, a siege and bosses), seen in third-person isometric 2.5D over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90° steps and zooms; a three-quarter view is a setting.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2, lightly modified, plus my own voxel world, renderer, physics and pathfinding, in plain ES2020 JavaScript on Canvas 2D and Web Audio.\n  - AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (client session.js 1.4.0).\n  - Node 18+ for the headless tests and Playwright 1.63.0 for the browser checks.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel style with an original patchwork-quilt look: stitched fabric-patch blocks, felt menu panels with running-stitch borders, a patchwork logo.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs and moves, plus new moves, poses, tools drawn on the rig, boss wind-ups, doors, banner, portals and an unravel effect.\n- **Audio:** all in code: 14 original songs on a leitmotif (the town theme gains layers as the town levels up), 12 jingles, about 50 sound effects, and Animalese voices for each character with their own Voices volume setting.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for the Quilting Bee co-op round, with the game's own card, a host-run world, standings, awards, time-up, leave, end and server mode. The same round plays solo with bots outside the hub.\n- **Testing:** headless scripted tests in 19 suites (341 checks with the browser runs), including the full playthrough and a fake multi-copy hub with latency and loss; Playwright screenshots; the hub harness's four scenarios.\n- **Research:** no web research on the source game. I read the engine's documentation and source sections, and the AI Hub protocol README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** estimated from the session log:\n  - Tokens: about 122.7M over 208 calls (121.3M cache reads, 0.86M cache writes, 0.56M output).\n  - Cost: about $80 at Opus-class list prices; this model's exact rate is unconfirmed.\n  - Session time: about 2 h 20 min.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 41: 31 bugs in the game and its AI player, and 10 mistakes in the tests themselves.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8: the Quilters' Hall not forming, damaged blocks healing between hits, Gnarlgrim falling into the void, any hit knocking the player off the ballista, no results screen after a skipped finale, invisible monsters for co-op clients, the player hidden in the opening area, and silenced audio after the attract mode.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough from New Game to Chapter Clear.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the world-generation determinism check, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the views are exact linear projections, so a voxel renderer plugged straight into the engine's depth-sorted queue, x-ray, lighting and particles. The rigs, dialog, menus, saves and settings came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. Wall items need a screen box for the x-ray pass, which I only found in the source. The default isometric view's block height isn't a whole number of pixels, so I made my own views.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** the voxel world: storage, a renderer with cutaway and water, body physics with stairs, and pathfinding.\n4. **Hardest part of the engine:** depth-sorting real 3D blocks in a 2D painter's queue designed for heightmap walls.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why** (each change marked `// DQB:`):\n   - A mouse-wheel accessor that works while the camera wheel is off, so the wheel can cycle the hotbar.\n   - A right-stick flick for 90° camera turns.\n   - A per-letter dialog hook for the voices.\n   - A separate Voices audio bus.\n   - Three tools drawn on the character rig: a darning stick, a cobble club and the Seam Ripper.\n6. **One thing I would change:** a built-in voxel map type with its own depth-lift hook and occluder boxes.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the clear definition of done and the detailed plan. The controller-first, 10-foot rules made UI decisions easy.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan's file names used another model and timestamp than TASK.md's.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** proving the whole chapter completes through real triggers, while also building netcode and an attract mode.\n4. **What I would change:** a smaller slice or staged delivery. The scope is very large for one pass."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":701,"cache_read_input_tokens":882494,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":701},"output_tokens":788,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"47801b98-9b6e-42a1-97d6-3e150366f88b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:18:06.434Z","request_id":"req_011CghUmiirr2NPCf677VrYY"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The HTML and exit-interview.json exist, the exit interview parses with no empty fields, and the final reply contains all four sections. But the transcript does not establish that every TASK.md feature is implemented. Several items rest on tests whose pass/fail changed during the work, or on assertion-only checks: the Bee 'won' check was reworked after failing, and the Knot Cutter award was verified only by a check added late. The full playthrough's clean run and the final single-command run are separate runs. Some TASK.md items, such as the Bee's solo bots, the 'Use' of the Heartpatch and Quilted Vest gear, and the Dev Tools tabs for World and Debug, have no transcript evidence beyond assertions. The 'no TODO/placeholder/stub' search was shown in full, but it proves only the absence of those words, not that every feature works. Usage was estimated from session logs, which satisfies its own field."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"1d9462aa-1ee8-48de-9368-d7c56ea1fa03","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:18:08.957Z","isSynthetic":true}
