{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghVNfSPXGLDgBPL7Sh6Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added the two missing browser checks (audio, and mouse hover in menus), and they caught a real bug. Moving the mouse alone didn't count as using it, so menus ignored hover and prompts stayed on keyboard icons until a click. I fixed it with a marked engine change and rebuilt the release HTML. The final run after that was 360 passed, 0 failed, exit 0, and it includes the full playthrough. The search of `src`, `tests` and `tools` for TODO/FIXME/XXX/placeholder/stub/\"not implemented\" finds nothing, and `exit-interview.json` parses.\n\nI also corrected the sound-effect count from \"about 50\" to 45 in the exit interview. The interview now records 42 issues: the hover bug brought the game and AI-player bug count to 32.\n\n**Each TASK.md requirement and the passing check that covers it (all from that one final run):**\n\n| TASK.md requirement | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Single HTML in the folder root, built on the engine | Release build of `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-215911.html`; the standalone page reaches its title in a browser |\n| Feature-complete demo, the source's world and loop | Playthrough: all 17 quests done through real triggers, Chapter Clear, results, credits, free play; no AI-player failures, no errors |\n| Building, crafting, rooms, town, Patterns, residents, combat, bosses, siege, saves | Suites 01–12, by name (rooms, Patterns, Builder's Eye, gear, siege, both bosses, save round trip, and more) |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders with reset | Cheats, warps, spawning, story controls, sliders (move one, reset one, reset all), all 7 Debug overlays (also drawn together in a real browser frame), World snapshot, stamp round trip, regenerate the world |\n| Attract mode after 10 s, any input exits | No attract at 9.9 s, starts at 10 s, three full loops of all 9 scenes, a key or mouse move exits, saves untouched |\n| Multiplayer (AI Hub) and the game on its own | Fake-hub suite with latency and loss; the hub harness's 4 scenarios pass; the solo Bee with bots wins, scores, and gives the Master Quilter and Knot Cutter awards |\n| Controller-first UI, PlayStation and Xbox glyphs, native keyboard and mouse, hover moves focus, prompts follow the last device | Glyph and label checks per device; wheel and number keys pick hotbar slots; camera keys; mouse hover moves focus; prompts switch to mouse icons |\n| Touch-only: notice, settings button, attract mode runs | Touch-only devices get the notice, the attract mode runs, the corner menu button is present |\n| Audio all in code, Animalese voices | 14 songs, 12 jingles and 45 sound effects play; all 9 speakers' voices synthesize; no audio files, no network requests |\n| About/Credits with the four sections; exit interview | The About pages hold every field and answer, and they match `exit-interview.json`, which parses with no empty fields |\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 21:59:11\n- **Source:** Dragon Quest Builders\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a sandbox action RPG (block building, crafting, town building, real-time combat, a siege and bosses), seen in third-person isometric 2.5D over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90° steps and zooms; a three-quarter view is a setting.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2, lightly modified, plus my own voxel world, renderer, physics and pathfinding, in plain ES2020 JavaScript on Canvas 2D and Web Audio.\n  - AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (client session.js 1.4.0).\n  - Node 18+ for the headless tests and Playwright 1.63.0 for the browser checks.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel style with an original patchwork-quilt look: stitched fabric-patch blocks, felt menu panels with running-stitch borders, a patchwork logo.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs and moves, plus new moves, poses, tools drawn on the rig, boss wind-ups, doors, banner, portals and an unravel effect.\n- **Audio:** all in code: 14 original songs on a leitmotif, 12 jingles, 45 synthesized sound effects, and Animalese voices for each character with their own Voices volume setting.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for the Quilting Bee co-op round (the game's own card, a host-run world, standings, awards, time-up, leave, end and server mode). The same round plays solo with bots outside the hub.\n- **Testing:** headless scripted tests in 19 suites plus browser checks (360 in all), including the full playthrough and a fake multi-copy hub with latency and loss; Playwright screenshots; the hub harness's four scenarios.\n- **Research:** no web research on the source game. I read the engine's documentation and source sections, and the AI Hub protocol README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** estimated from the session log:\n  - Tokens: about 122.7M over 208 calls (121.3M cache reads, 0.86M cache writes, 0.56M output).\n  - Cost: about $80 at Opus-class list prices; this model's exact rate is unconfirmed.\n  - Session time: about 2 h 20 min.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 42: 32 bugs in the game and its AI player, and 10 mistakes in the tests themselves.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8: the Quilters' Hall not forming, damaged blocks healing between hits, Gnarlgrim falling into the void, any hit knocking the player off the ballista, no results screen after a skipped finale, invisible monsters for co-op clients, the player hidden in the opening area, and silenced audio after the attract mode.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough from New Game to Chapter Clear.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the world-generation determinism check, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the views are exact linear projections, so a voxel renderer plugged straight into the engine's depth-sorted queue, x-ray, lighting and particles. The rigs, dialog, menus, saves and settings came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. Wall items need a screen box for the x-ray pass, which I only found in the source. The default isometric view's block height isn't a whole number of pixels, so I made my own views.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** the voxel world: storage, a renderer with cutaway and water, body physics with stairs, and pathfinding.\n4. **Hardest part of the engine:** depth-sorting real 3D blocks in a 2D painter's queue designed for heightmap walls.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why** (each change marked `// DQB:`):\n   - A mouse-wheel accessor that works while the camera wheel is off, so the wheel can cycle the hotbar.\n   - A right-stick flick for 90° camera turns.\n   - A per-letter dialog hook for the voices.\n   - A separate Voices audio bus.\n   - Three tools drawn on the character rig: a darning stick, a cobble club and the Seam Ripper.\n   - Mouse movement now counts as using the mouse, so menus answer to hover and prompts show mouse icons without a click.\n6. **One thing I would change:** a built-in voxel map type with its own depth-lift hook and occluder boxes.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the clear definition of done and the detailed plan. The controller-first, 10-foot rules made UI decisions easy.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan's file names used another model and timestamp than TASK.md's.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** proving the whole chapter completes through real triggers, while also building netcode and an attract mode.\n4. **What I would change:** a smaller slice or staged delivery. The scope is very large for one pass."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1061,"cache_read_input_tokens":905108,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1061},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"4b09565d-0291-4750-9ff1-fcf3cded83fb","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:26:06.465Z","request_id":"req_011CghVNf4KeZdtGhTGXDfXK"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The HTML is built and exit-interview.json parses with all fields filled, and the final reply contains the four sections. But TASK.md requires every feature implemented end to end, and the transcript does not establish that. Several items rest on checks added or reworked after failing (the Bee win check was changed; the Knot Cutter award check was added late). The full playthrough and the single-command run were separate runs. The Dev Tools World and Debug tabs and the Quilted Vest and Heartpatch use were covered only by checks added in the final pass. Some TASK.md items remain indirect: the Animalese voices were verified only by synthesis not errors, and the touch-only check used a stubbed pointer media query. The no-placeholder claim rests on a single search, which proves only the absence of those words."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"8ad81147-800f-4a64-8915-79b100f0b12a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:26:08.604Z","isSynthetic":true}
