{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghVqxSbJJVx5KZuwyoig","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I closed the last two gaps with real measurements, not stubs. The final single run after the last edit was 367 passed, 0 failed, exit 0, and it includes all 67 playthrough checks. The search of `src`, `tests` and `tools` finds no TODO/FIXME/XXX/placeholder/stub, and `exit-interview.json` parses.\n\n- **Voices are audible:** an analyser on the Voices volume channel measured real signal for every one of the 9 speakers, and the levels differ between them.\n- **Touch-only, on an emulated phone:** this used Playwright's touch emulation (coarse pointer, no keyboard or controller) instead of a faked media query.\n  - The phone gets the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice, with the settings and menu buttons; I checked a screenshot.\n  - It shows no touch controls.\n  - The attract mode starts after 10 seconds, and a tap leaves it.\n- **A test-harness problem that run exposed:** in the first combined run, the new browser check crashed before closing its browser, which starved the hub harness that runs next. The game wasn't at fault; each check passed on its own. The browser check now always closes its browser on failure, and the repeated full run passed everything.\n\nThe game file `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-215911.html` and `exit-interview.json` are in the work folder.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 21:59:11\n- **Source:** Dragon Quest Builders\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a sandbox action RPG (block building, crafting, town building, real-time combat, a siege and bosses), seen in third-person isometric 2.5D over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90° steps and zooms; a three-quarter view is a setting.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2, lightly modified, plus my own voxel world, renderer, physics and pathfinding, in plain ES2020 JavaScript on Canvas 2D and Web Audio.\n  - AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (client session.js 1.4.0).\n  - Node 18+ for the headless tests and Playwright 1.63.0 for the browser checks.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel style with an original patchwork-quilt look: stitched fabric-patch blocks, felt menu panels with running-stitch borders, a patchwork logo.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs and moves, plus new moves, poses, tools drawn on the rig, boss wind-ups, doors, banner, portals and an unravel effect.\n- **Audio:** all in code: 14 original songs on a leitmotif, 12 jingles, 45 synthesized sound effects, and Animalese voices for each character with their own Voices volume setting.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for the Quilting Bee co-op round (the game's own card, a host-run world, standings, awards, time-up, leave, end and server mode). The same round plays solo with bots outside the hub.\n- **Testing:** headless scripted tests in 19 suites plus browser checks (367 in all), including the full playthrough, a fake multi-copy hub with latency and loss, an emulated touch phone and audio level measurement; Playwright screenshots; the hub harness's four scenarios.\n- **Research:** no web research on the source game. I read the engine's documentation and source sections, and the AI Hub protocol README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** estimated from the session log:\n  - Tokens: about 122.7M over 208 calls (121.3M cache reads, 0.86M cache writes, 0.56M output).\n  - Cost: about $80 at Opus-class list prices; this model's exact rate is unconfirmed.\n  - Session time: about 2 h 20 min.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 42: 32 bugs in the game and its AI player, and 10 mistakes in the tests themselves.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8: the Quilters' Hall not forming, damaged blocks healing between hits, Gnarlgrim falling into the void, any hit knocking the player off the ballista, no results screen after a skipped finale, invisible monsters for co-op clients, the player hidden in the opening area, and silenced audio after the attract mode.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough from New Game to Chapter Clear.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the world-generation determinism check, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the views are exact linear projections, so a voxel renderer plugged straight into the engine's depth-sorted queue, x-ray, lighting and particles. The rigs, dialog, menus, saves and settings came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. Wall items need a screen box for the x-ray pass, which I only found in the source. The default isometric view's block height isn't a whole number of pixels, so I made my own views.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** the voxel world: storage, a renderer with cutaway and water, body physics with stairs, and pathfinding.\n4. **Hardest part of the engine:** depth-sorting real 3D blocks in a 2D painter's queue designed for heightmap walls.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why** (each change marked `// DQB:`):\n   - A mouse-wheel accessor that works while the camera wheel is off, so the wheel can cycle the hotbar.\n   - A right-stick flick for 90° camera turns.\n   - A per-letter dialog hook for the voices.\n   - A separate Voices audio bus.\n   - Three tools drawn on the character rig: a darning stick, a cobble club and the Seam Ripper.\n   - Mouse movement now counts as using the mouse, so menus answer to hover and prompts show mouse icons without a click.\n6. **One thing I would change:** a built-in voxel map type with its own depth-lift hook and occluder boxes.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the clear definition of done and the detailed plan. The controller-first, 10-foot rules made UI decisions easy.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan's file names used another model and timestamp than TASK.md's.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** proving the whole chapter completes through real triggers, while also building netcode and an attract mode.\n4. **What I would change:** a smaller slice or staged delivery. The scope is very large for one pass."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1703,"cache_read_input_tokens":915546,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1703},"output_tokens":763,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"13536467-e807-4411-946a-d3d1c675792b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:32:11.035Z","request_id":"req_011CghVqx1HneHkLwr59jK6L"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The HTML was rebuilt and exit-interview.json parses with every field filled, and the final reply has all four sections. But the transcript does not establish that every TASK.md feature is implemented and verified end to end. The final single run is the last recorded one (367 passed, 0 failed), yet the earlier run in the same final pass exited 1 with 5 failures (4 hub harness and 1 features check), and that failure was attributed to a harness problem rather than shown to be fixed under the same run. Several TASK.md items rest on checks added or reworked after failing, including the Bee win and the Knot Cutter award. The Usage field is an estimate from session logs, and the Animalese voices and touch-only notice are checked by synthesis, audio-level measurement and an emulated phone rather than by inspecting the feature in play. The placeholder search proves only that those words are absent. Per the condition's requirement that every feature be implemented and verifiable from the transcript, completion is not clearly established."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"10729513-0289-454d-b5ca-9babbe9c4fc2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:32:13.673Z","isSynthetic":true}
