 by passing checks in this run (367 passed, 0 failed)\ncoverage exit 0","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"ca9b77c4-0eb3-4e02-9dad-f84d11c1a840","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:42:47.547Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"PASS 14-attract: the attract mode never touches the saves\nPASS 17-bee: awards name the Knot Cutter: the player with the most kills\nCOVERED  Package: one bundled HTML in the folder root, works on its own  [1 named checks + file check]\nCOVERED  Genre and perspective: building RPG in an isometric voxel world, turning camera  [3 named checks]\nCOVERED  Tech stack built on the engine (rigs, physics, rendering hooks)  [2 named checks]\nCOVERED  Source's world and loop: gather, craft, build rooms, town levels, residents  [5 named checks]\nCOVERED  Combat, monsters, bosses, siege  [5 named checks]\nCOVERED  Demo long enough to show all features, complete end to end  [4 named checks]\nCOVERED  Infrastructure for later content: saves, story beats, stamps  [3 named checks]\nCOVERED  Dev mode from the pause menu with standard test tools  [7 named checks]\nCOVERED  Live balance sliders with reset to defaults  [6 named checks]\nCOVERED  Attract mode after 10 s idle, looping, any input exits  [6 named checks]\nCOVERED  Multiplayer per the AI Hub protocol, and the game on its own  [7 named checks]\nCOVERED  Controller first: PlayStation glyphs, Xbox option  [3 named checks]\nCOVERED  Keyboard and mouse native; hover moves focus; prompts follow the last device  [5 named checks]\nCOVERED  10-foot UI screens work by menu actions  [3 named checks]\nCOVERED  Touch-only: notice, settings button, no touch controls, attract runs  [5 named checks]\nCOVERED  Audio all in code; Animalese voices  [4 named checks]\nCOVERED  Narrative tone, font-safe text  [1 named checks]\nCOVERED  In-game About/Credits with the four sections  [3 named checks]\nCOVERED  exit-interview.json parses and answers every field  [0 named checks + file check]\nCOVERED  Testing: one command, only failures and a count line  [0 named checks + file check]\nevery TASK.md requirement is covered by passing checks in this run (367 passed, 0 failed)\ncoverage exit 0","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UqaQHzEfNeARgQ8ptZXjH1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghWg8sjDVKqfCQAiJWbj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I checked every TASK.md requirement against one fresh full run of the test command: all 20 are covered by named checks that passed, or by a direct file check. That run was 367 passed, 0 failed, exit 0. I wrote `tools/coverage.mjs` to map each requirement to its checks and confirm each one passed.\n\nThe first coverage pass flagged two requirements as missing. That was my map's fault: two patterns didn't match the checks' exact names. Both checks (\"the attract mode never touches the saves\" and \"awards name the Knot Cutter\") had passed. After I fixed the patterns, the same run output shows all 20 covered. No game or test code changed after that run.\n\n| Requirement | Named checks |\n|---|---|\n| Single HTML in the folder root, works on its own | 1 + file check |\n| Genre and perspective | 3 |\n| Built on the engine | 2 |\n| The source's world and loop | 5 |\n| Combat, bosses and siege | 5 |\n| Complete demo, end to end | 4 |\n| Infrastructure for later content | 3 |\n| Dev mode tools | 7 |\n| Live sliders with reset | 6 |\n| Attract mode | 6 |\n| Multiplayer, and the game on its own | 7 |\n| Controller glyphs (PlayStation, Xbox) | 3 |\n| Keyboard and mouse, hover, prompts | 5 |\n| 10-foot menus | 3 |\n| Touch-only devices | 5 |\n| Audio in code, Animalese voices | 4 |\n| Font-safe text | 1 |\n| About/Credits | 3 |\n| Exit interview | file check |\n| One-command testing | file check |\n\nTwo limits remain. The touch-only notice was checked on Playwright's emulated phone, not a physical device, and the voices were measured in play in a desktop browser. I confirmed both by screenshot.\n\nThe TODO/FIXME/XXX/placeholder/stub search finds nothing. The game file `DragonQuiltBuilders-DragonQuestBuilders-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-215911.html` and `exit-interview.json` are in the work folder; the exit interview parses with every field filled.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 21:59:11\n- **Source:** Dragon Quest Builders\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a sandbox action RPG (block building, crafting, town building, real-time combat, a siege and bosses), seen in third-person isometric 2.5D over a true voxel world. The camera turns in 90° steps and zooms; a three-quarter view is a setting.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2, lightly modified, plus my own voxel world, renderer, physics and pathfinding, in plain ES2020 JavaScript on Canvas 2D and Web Audio.\n  - AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (client session.js 1.4.0).\n  - Node 18+ for the headless tests and Playwright 1.63.0 for the browser checks.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel style with an original patchwork-quilt look: stitched fabric-patch blocks, felt menu panels with running-stitch borders, a patchwork logo.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs and moves, plus new moves, poses, tools drawn on the rig, boss wind-ups, doors, banner, portals and an unravel effect.\n- **Audio:** all in code: 14 original songs on a leitmotif, 12 jingles, 45 synthesized sound effects, and Animalese voices for each character with their own Voices volume setting.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for the Quilting Bee co-op round. The same round plays solo with bots outside the hub.\n- **Testing:** headless scripted tests in 19 suites plus browser checks (367 in all), including the full playthrough, a fake multi-copy hub with latency and loss, an emulated touch phone, and audio levels measured during real play; a coverage map from TASK.md requirements to checks; Playwright screenshots; the hub harness's four scenarios.\n- **Research:** no web research on the source game. I read the engine's documentation and source sections, and the AI Hub protocol README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** estimated from the session log:\n  - Tokens: about 122.7M over 208 calls (121.3M cache reads, 0.86M cache writes, 0.56M output).\n  - Cost: about $80 at Opus-class list prices; this model's exact rate is unconfirmed.\n  - Session time: about 2 h 20 min.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 42: 32 bugs in the game and its AI player, and 10 mistakes in the tests themselves.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8: the Quilters' Hall not forming, damaged blocks healing between hits, Gnarlgrim falling into the void, any hit knocking the player off the ballista, no results screen after a skipped finale, invisible monsters for co-op clients, the player hidden in the opening area, and silenced audio after the attract mode.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough from New Game to Chapter Clear.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the world-generation determinism check, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the views are exact linear projections, so a voxel renderer plugged straight into the engine's depth-sorted queue, x-ray, lighting and particles. The rigs, dialog, menus, saves and settings came free.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. Wall items need a screen box for the x-ray pass, which I only found in the source. The default isometric view's block height isn't a whole number of pixels, so I made my own views.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** the voxel world: storage, a renderer with cutaway and water, body physics with stairs, and pathfinding.\n4. **Hardest part of the engine:** depth-sorting real 3D blocks in a 2D painter's queue designed for heightmap walls.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why** (each change marked `// DQB:`):\n   - A mouse-wheel accessor that works while the camera wheel is off, so the wheel can cycle the hotbar.\n   - A right-stick flick for 90° camera turns.\n   - A per-letter dialog hook for the voices.\n   - A separate Voices audio bus.\n   - Three tools drawn on the character rig: a darning stick, a cobble club and the Seam Ripper.\n   - Mouse movement now counts as using the mouse, so menus answer to hover and prompts show mouse icons without a click.\n6. **One thing I would change:** a built-in voxel map type with its own depth-lift hook and occluder boxes.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the clear definition of done and the detailed plan. The controller-first, 10-foot rules made UI decisions easy.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan's file names used another model and timestamp than TASK.md's.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** proving the whole chapter completes through real triggers, while also building netcode and an attract mode.\n4. **What I would change:** a smaller slice or staged delivery. The scope is very large for one pass."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1245,"cache_read_input_tokens":941011,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1245},"output_tokens":807,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"99945d60-cc75-4209-83e6-a9ee31f7135d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:43:05.769Z","request_id":"req_011CghWg8SgVTtTEqosSY2mu"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The HTML exists and exit-interview.json parses with every field answered, and the final reply has all four sections. But the transcript does not establish that every TASK.md feature is implemented and verified. The coverage map was built from the same test run, so it shows that named checks passed, not that every requirement works end to end. The first coverage pass flagged two requirements as missing and was fixed by changing the pattern matching, which the assistant describes as a mapping error. The Bee win and Knot Cutter checks were added or reworked after failing. Several TASK.md items (the touch-only notice, the Animalese voices, the Usage field) were verified only by emulation, audio-level measurement or estimation, not by inspecting the feature on a real device. TASK.md also asks for Usage estimates when available, and the estimate rests on session-log data the transcript does not show being verified."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7d7e0cb-a26c-4fe8-90f7-7d5024813af2","uuid":"efae295c-47fb-4ed8-8d5a-460cbbabf23a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:43:07.968Z","isSynthetic":true}
